                <pb facs="0635" n="517" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.512/870: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-10-30T09:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 30,
                                1928—9 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 10:45
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>802. Department’s 360, October 26, 5 p.m. Following from consul general at
                    Canton: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Gunboat has not gone alongside, and I am still working with the Chinese
                            authorities as indicated in my October 23, 2 p.m.</p>
                        <p>2. In view of our treaty rights I considered action of Chinese (other
                            than Maritime Customs) in forcibly seizing bona fide American vessels as
                            an outrage to our flag.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
